{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"aaaf1f6c-362b-47da-beae-f93570809cf2","step":{"id":"s1","script":"We want the area under this curve from zero to two. Rectangles give us an approximation we can calculate easily.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Area from rectangles"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^2,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le 2"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.1,2.2],"y":[-0.5,4.6],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"f(x)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"curve","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2]},{"kind":"shade","under":"curve","from":0,"to":2,"label":"area"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"aaaf1f6c-362b-47da-beae-f93570809cf2","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Split the interval into four equal pieces. Use each right endpoint to choose its rectangle’s height.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"rectangles","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0,0],[0.5,0],[0.5,0.25],[0,0.25]],"closed":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0.5,0],[1,0],[1,1],[0.5,1]],"closed":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1,0],[1.5,0],[1.5,2.25],[1,2.25]],"closed":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1.5,0],[2,0],[2,4],[1.5,4]],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"curve","x":0.5}},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"curve","x":1}},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"curve","x":1.5}},{"kind":"point","at":{"on":"curve","x":2}}]},{"op":"equation","id":"width","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\Delta x=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"aaaf1f6c-362b-47da-beae-f93570809cf2","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Add width times height for all four rectangles. Because the curve increases, these right-endpoint rectangles overestimate the area. Finer partitions improve the approximation.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"widthmark","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[1.5,-0.25],"to":[2,-0.25],"label":"0.5"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.75,3.0625],"to":[1.75,4],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.6,3.6],"text":"Overestimate","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"width","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"R_4=0.5(0.25+1+2.25+4)=3.75"},{"op":"equation","id":"general","anchor":{"el":"sum","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"R_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i)\\,\\Delta x"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For this increasing curve, would left-endpoint rectangles overestimate or underestimate the area?","expected_gist":"Underestimate: each rectangle lies below the curve."}}}
